# `__modname__` business module

`weld add module __modname__` generated this package. weld writes it once and
records every file in `weld.json`; it does not regenerate them, so your edits
survive later `weld add` commands.

## What it is

An HTTP business module wired under `GET /api/__modname__`:

- `dto.go` — the typed `Request` and `Response`.
- `service.go` — the `Service` interface, the default `NewService`, and the
  `NewDemoService` demo constructor the package's tests use.
- `module.go` — `operations()`, `Register` and the HTTP handler.
- `contract.go` — the OpenAPI contract for those routes, registered with the
  shared `internal/openapi` registry.

The handler writes through `internal/httpx`, the shared toolkit: `httpx.JSON`
wraps a value in the `{code, msg, data}` envelope and `httpx.Error` writes an
error envelope with `data: null`. Use `httpx.RawText`, `httpx.Bytes` or
`httpx.NoContent` for a response that must stay unwrapped.

## Routes and the OpenAPI document

`operations()` in `module.go` is the single route table: `Register` installs each
entry on the composed mux and `greetingContract` in `contract.go` documents the
same entry. The contract is registered with `internal/openapi` when the package
is initialized, so `weld add api` includes these routes in
`GET /api/openapi.json` without the api package importing this one. The success
schema is rendered from this package's own `Response` DTO, so the wire shape and
the document cannot drift.

Add an endpoint by adding an entry to `operations()` and its contract to
`contract.go` together. The contract is explicit on purpose: weld never infers a
route, a request shape or an authorization rule from a handler, so a customized
module documents exactly what its author declares. A module that predates this
contract simply contributes no documented operation; it never breaks the build.

## Replaceable example, not persistence

The default `Service` is an in-memory development demo. It writes nothing to
disk and keeps no state across restarts. Replace the body of `NewService` (or
return your own implementation) to add real behaviour. Keep the `Service`
interface and the `NewService` symbol stable.

## How it is wired

Generating this module installed `http` and `loom` (and the `config` they
require), and wrote the shared `internal/openapi` contract package. The generated
Loom server graph in `internal/di` provides `__modname__.NewService` and
registers the module on the composed mux (`di.newMux`), so you may change
`NewService`'s signature — for example to take `data.Repository` after
`weld add db`. Nothing in this package quotes the graph, and no separate route
file is written.

To change the wire format or add endpoints, edit this package; the route table
and its contract live in `module.go` and `contract.go`.
